Find how the framework is *meant* to be driven offline before writing anything, and go
looking rather than waiting to be told. Where to look, in order:

 - the installed package under {{ python }}'s site-packages -- its examples, its tests, and
   its `*.dist-info/METADATA`, which records the project's own repository URL;
 - a source checkout of the framework on this machine. A project often keeps its per-model
   recipes and examples in its git repo and not in its wheel, so the installed package can
   be missing exactly the page you need;
 - the repository itself, if you can reach it.

What you are looking for first is a recipe or example for **{{ model }} on this card**. One
often exists, and it states the entry point, the precision and the settings the model needs
to run at all -- which is most of what a launcher is.

Failing that, take the nearest, in this order, and note that the same model on another card
beats a different model on this one -- getting the call wrong stops the run, while a wrong
memory setting is something you can measure and change:

 1. {{ model }} on another device. The entry point and the pipeline class are exactly
    right. The precision and offloading are not: they were sized to that card's memory.
 2. another size of {{ model }}, on any device -- same family, so the call holds.
 3. another model of the same family, then any model of the same kind under this framework.
    Here only the convention carries: how a pipeline class is named, which settings exist.

What transfers is the shape of the call. What does not is anything sized to a model or a
card: the precision, the offloading, the resolution, the step count -- and the workload
above is what this run measures, so it is not a recipe's to set. Follow a neighbour's form
and establish the numbers here. Say in "notes" which recipe you worked from and what you
changed, so a reader knows which parts were borrowed.


A framework's entry point usually takes one shape and refuses every near miss: an argument
that looks obvious from a signature is often the wrong one, and a type annotation does not
tell you how the pieces are built. Copy a working call and change the parts you need. If
you find nothing, say so in "notes" -- the launcher then rests on your reading of the
source and is worth more doubt.

