Before downloading or running anything large, decide whether this fits the device: the
model's config (hub metadata, no weights), the device's memory, and how the framework runs
it at this workload. If it cannot run here -- it does not fit, or it is not a
{% if workload == "vision" %}model that generates an image from a text prompt{% else %}text decoder that runs on token ids{% endif %} --
write {"fits": false, "reason": "<one line>"} as {{ answer }} and stop.

Memory is the usual reason it does not fit, and there are two different answers to it.
Weight offloading you may turn on yourself: it moves weights between host and device and
changes neither the numbers the model computes nor the kernels it runs, only the copies
around them. Say in "notes" that you did.

{% if quantization %}Run at {{ quantization }} precision, which is what was asked for.
{% else %}Run at the model's own precision.{% endif %} **Do not choose a precision to make a
model fit.** A reduced precision changes what the model produces, and whether that is
acceptable is a judgement about output quality that you cannot make here; it also changes
which kernels run, so optimizing it would tune kernels that a differently-served model never
executes. If the model only fits at a lower precision, that is a {"fits": false} with the
reason saying so -- name the precision that would fit, so a person can decide and pass it. A framework that runs a converted model (an exported IR, an engine) needs it converted
once: into {{ cache }}/models/<framework>/<model id with / as -->, reused when it is there.

If the framework has no implementation of this architecture on this device (a generic
fallback, such as a transformers backend, is none), or an op the model needs fails, runs on
the CPU, or is emulated in plain torch in place of a kernel (a portable Triton kernel is a
kernel), do not work around it: write {"unsupported": "model" or "op", "what": "<the
architecture or op>", "reason": "<file:line where the framework shows it>"} as {{ answer }}
and stop. A patch that adds it is established first, and you are run again on it. If
{{ python }}'s build of the framework departs from what its own docs say to use on this
device (a build option, a backend), measure it as it is and name the departure in "notes".

Write launcher/run.sh here: an executable that runs that workload once more with every
setting it needs, the framework under $FRAMEWORK_PYTHON when that is set (it is when another
build of the stack is measured) and {{ python }} otherwise, and writes into $RAW_DIR:
 - meta.json: {"framework", "framework_version", "model": {"name": "{{ model }}" exactly as
   given here, verbatim -- not the id you resolved it to, not the directory you found it in;
   put that in "resolved" if you want it recorded, "config": <its config as
   a dict>}, "runtime": {{ '{' }}{% for k in runtime_keys %}"{{ k }}", {% endfor %}and every setting that changes
   what runs}, "device": {"type", "name"}, "repeats": <how many times the process ran the
   identical workload, warm-up included>};
 - for a framework that runs on PyTorch: its torch.profiler trace of exactly the generation
   (trace*.json.gz, record_shapes=True; `xe_forge.frontend.profile.profile_region(fn,
   raw_dir)` does this for a callable in this process -- a framework that runs the model in
   another process profiles it there, with its own profiler, into $RAW_DIR);
 - for any other framework: ops.json, every node that ran on the device:
   {"<node>": {"framework_op": "<its type>", "kernels": ["<regex over the kernel names
   unitrace reports>"], "args": [{"name", "shape", "dtype": <torch's name: float16, int8,
   ...>}], "call_site": "<node name>",
   "calls": <runs in one workload>}} with concrete shapes, read from the framework's own
   runtime API; which kernels a node ran is what you establish, not guess from names. When
   one kernel name serves several nodes (a library's GEMM for every matmul), give each node
   "launches": [[start, end], ...] as well -- index ranges into raw/unitrace_launches.json,
   the measured run's launches in device order, which `capture run` writes: run it once,
   read that, and have the launcher write the ranges. That file comes from the instrumented
   pass below, which XE_FORGE_TRACE_STEPS can make shorter than the workload asked for, so
   count the ranges in that pass and not in the full run.

`capture run` runs your launcher twice: once plain, which is the measurement, and once under
unitrace, which only names the kernels and reads the counters. For the second it sets
**XE_FORGE_TRACE_STEPS**, and your launcher has to read it.

This workload repeats one thing many times -- {% if workload == "vision" %}{{ steps }} denoising steps over the same
transformer{% else %}{{ output_len }} decode steps over the same layers{% endif %}. When XE_FORGE_TRACE_STEPS is in the environment, do that many
repetitions instead, and change nothing else: same prompt, same seed, {% if workload == "vision" %}same size{% else %}same prompt length{% endif %},
same number of repeats of the whole workload. Record what you actually ran in meta.json's
runtime -- `capture run` compares the two passes' runtime to tell whether you read the
variable at all, and says so when you did not. Write the output and the trace in both passes
as usual; the instrumented pass's copies are discarded.

Honour it even though it looks like it spoils the measurement, because it does not. The
timed pass keeps the whole workload, and that pass is the measurement. A few repetitions
still run every kernel the full workload runs, because the shortened run is complete end to
end -- it still {% if workload == "vision" %}encodes the prompt and decodes the image{% else %}prefills and samples{% endif %} -- so nothing goes
unnamed; only the call counts fall. What falls with them is the thing that breaks this pass:
unitrace puts a timing event on every launch and the driver's supply of them is finite, so a
long workload can exhaust it part way through and lose the whole pass -- after the model has
been loaded and run.

Do not shorten anything that runs once -- the prompt encode, the decode of the image, the
prefill. Those are a share of the time too, and cutting them would hide kernels.

{% if workload == "vision" %}Save what the model produced into $RAW_DIR, beside the trace: an image as
`output.png`, a video as `output.mp4`, its audio as `output.wav` if it has any. Write the
frames at the frame rate the model used, not as separate files.

This is not optional and it is not for the measurement. Nothing downstream can tell whether
a replaced kernel still makes an acceptable picture -- a kernel is kept on a numeric
tolerance and a timing comparison, neither of which looks at the output. A person has to,
and they cannot if the run decoded a video and threw it away. A capture that writes no
output leaves every patch from this run unjudgeable.

{% endif %}Your launcher is run again later, and not only here. The optimize step re-runs it to
compare a patched build against an unpatched one, in a freshly built environment with
nothing in the page cache. So it has to work cold, not just now: give every start-up or
stage-initialisation timeout the framework offers enough room to load this model's weights
off a network filesystem from cold, and prefer a generous value to the default. A timeout
that was ample during this capture -- when the weights were already warm from your own
earlier attempts -- is the commonest way that later comparison fails, and it fails after
the whole build has been paid for.

And it has to **exit cleanly**, because its exit code is the whole verdict: a launcher that
generated its output and wrote its whole trace, and then died on the way out, is recorded as
a failed launcher and its work is thrown away. Shut the framework down explicitly and wait
for it. A framework that hands its own shutdown to a background thread and returns will
abort the process if you exit while that thread runs, and whether it does is a race -- so it
can pass here and fail in the comparison, or pass for one build and fail for the other.
Check what its shutdown actually does rather than assuming the call is synchronous.

Then run, under any interpreter that imports xe_forge (PYTHONPATH is set):
  xe-forge-skill capture run launcher/run.sh -o raw
  xe-forge-skill capture parse raw -o capture.json
`capture run` runs the launcher twice, the second time under unitrace, and records every
kernel the device ran (raw/unitrace_kernels.json) with its counters. `capture parse` builds
capture.json and checks it: exit 0 passes; exit 1 prints each PROBLEM. Fix the launcher and
run both again until parse passes. Report the numbers it prints; do not edit raw/ or
capture.json yourself.

Write {{ answer }}: {"launcher": "launcher/run.sh", "capture": "capture.json", "notes":
"<what the launcher does and why, one paragraph>"}, or {"error": "<why it cannot be
captured>", "next_step": "<what a person would do>"}.
